{"text": " or a number the entire financial system is anchored to, you'd assume it fell out of some impossibly complex math equation, or it was carved in stone and passed down generations. Well, neither. Somebody chose it. So why the 2%? If inflation's the thing quietly making everything more expensive, why aim for any of it at all? Because while on paper a 0% inflation rate sounds great, in reality, it's not. When a recession hits, the Fed's main move is to cut interest rates. In a world with zero inflation, rates already sit near the floor. So the second a real economic downturn arrives, the Fed gets one cut, maybe two, and then slams into zero, structurally trapped and out of road. That's how an ordinary recession can spiral into a decade-long depression. But you can't aim too high either. Push it to 4% and prices double every 18 years. Businesses can't forecast their cost, and nobody can price a long-term contract without guessing. So 2% is that Goldilocks number, high enough to keep a cushion between the economy and catastrophe, low enough that you barely notice it on an ordinary Tuesday.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 2.98, "text": " or a number the entire financial system is anchored to,", "tokens": [50363, 393, 257, 1271, 262, 2104, 3176, 1080, 318, 39871, 284, 11, 50524], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1638932716189407, "compression_ratio": 1.549342105263158, "no_speech_prob": 0.0014998061815276742, "words": [{"word": " or", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.12, "probability": 0.2961135506629944}, {"word": " a", "start": 0.12, "end": 0.26, "probability": 0.976688802242279}, {"word": " number", "start": 0.26, "end": 0.48, "probability": 0.9966815114021301}, {"word": " the", "start": 0.48, "end": 0.72, "probability": 0.983742356300354}, {"word": " entire", "start": 0.72, "end": 1.08, "probability": 0.9970366954803467}, {"word": " financial", "start": 1.08, "end": 1.48, "probability": 0.9949665665626526}, {"word": " system", "start": 1.48